{
  "_comment": [
    "THE RULER FOR SEARCH. Without this, 'better search' is a feeling.",
    "",
    "Each query names domains that ANSWER it and domains that are a wrong turn.",
    "The wrong turns are not hypothetical — every one listed under `bad` was",
    "actually returned by a live engine for that exact query, which is what makes",
    "them worth measuring. `poison@3` is the metric that catches the failure that",
    "started this: on 23 Aug 2026 Bing answered 'best laptops of 2026' with four",
    "dictionary definitions of the word 'best', and nothing in the pipeline noticed",
    "because the search had technically succeeded.",
    "",
    "`good` is deliberately a list of SITES rather than exact URLs. Exact URLs rot",
    "within weeks and would turn this into a test of whether a page has been",
    "renamed. A site that publishes the answer is a stable enough target to compare",
    "two rankings against.",
    "",
    "THE RULE FOR ADDING TO `good`, because otherwise this stops being a ruler.",
    "",
    "The first version of these lists was written from memory and was wrong: it",
    "marked 'MySQL vs PostgreSQL: The Write-Heavy Showdown' a MISS for the",
    "postgres-vs-mysql query, because medium.com had not been listed. Three of the",
    "twelve queries were punishing correct answers, and tuning a ranker against",
    "that would have been tuning it towards the mistake.",
    "",
    "So a site may be added ONLY when the page it returned for THIS query has a",
    "title that states the query's intent outright — 'How to Stop Windows 11 From",
    "Reopening Apps After Restart' for the windows query. It may NOT be added",
    "because the current implementation happened to return it. That distinction is",
    "the whole difference between correcting a ruler and bending one.",
    "",
    "Queries are phrased the way the agent actually phrases them — taken from real",
    "transcripts, including the sloppy ones. A benchmark of well-formed queries",
    "measures a search engine nobody is using."
  ],
